    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. checking the probes now. i'll also look at the resource limits like you said. one thing though—i just noticed that the config file being read is actually pulling from a secret that's mounted as a volume. if the EOF error is happening right at startup, could there be a race condition where the app tries to read the secret before the volume is fully mounted by k8s? how would i verify that specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A race condition involving volume mounts and startup logic is a classic, tricky problem. You can't always see the actual timing, but you can force the application to wait until the necessary resources are ready.\n\nThe most direct way to verify this is to introduce a startup delay or, better yet, use an `initContainer`. An `initContainer` runs to completion *before* your main application container starts, giving it time to ensure all necessary volumes are mounted and ready. You can use a simple `sleep` command in the init container, or even better, use a script that actively checks for the file's existence before proceeding.\n\nIf you suspect the secret mount is the culprit, you should review your application's startup sequence to see if it has a built-in readiness check for its configuration files. If it doesn't, the `initContainer` is your best bet to act as a reliable gatekeeper.",
      "source": "target",
